{{ macros.terminology("A **module** is a named, namespaced bundle of variables and targets, brought into scope from an inline block or a file with a single declaration.  The source is never mutated: a prefixed, optionally-compiled *copy* is staged and included, so imports never collide and one body can be brought in several ways.", title="**Terminology: Module**") }}

A **module**[^module] is a named bundle of variables and targets brought into scope with a single declaration.  Modules give you three things at once:

* **Namespacing:** every variable and target is prefixed with the module's name, so modules never collide.
* **Non-destructive import:** the source (an inline block or a file) is never mutated; a namespaced, optionally-compiled *copy* is staged and included.
* **An optional compile step:** the body is run through a pipeline (default: the [CMK compiler]({{_}}/cmk/compiler)) before it is brought into scope.

{% include "includes/module-algebra.md" %}

### From Code {% raw %}{#declaring-a-module}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

A module is written in two parts: a named [banana block]({{_}}/cmk/banana) captures the body, then a single call stages and imports it.

```cmk {.snippet .language-cmk}
MyModule[|
  var1 := val1
  greet:; @echo hello from ${CMK_MODULE}
|]

cmk.import.module(def=MyModule)
```

This stages the module and exposes its members under the module's name (`MyModule.var1`, `MyModule.greet`, and so on).  Nothing is imported until that call, so one body can be brought in several ways: the [Module Algebra](#module-algebra) card at the top of the page lists the declaration and every import variation at a glance.

The import call takes a few keywords, each also in that card:

* **namespace** renames the module on import (the member prefix only; a recipe's own identity always stays its source name).  Omitted, it defaults to the module's own name.
* **preprocs** picks the [import hooks](#import-hooks).  Omitted, it defaults to the CMK compiler.
* A docstring on the first line of the body becomes the module's documentation on import.

The demo defines a module with a couple of targets, then runs them by their namespaced names:

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/module-system.mk', 'demos/cmk/module-system.cmk',
    github=github, default='cmk')}}

### Instance-method form {% raw %}{#module-import-instance-method}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

A `module NAME[| .. |]` declaration is a `cmk.module` instance, and every instance carries an `.import` bound-method: a partial over `import.module` that fills in its own `def=`.  So the whole-module import has an instance spelling, bound to the module's own name.  The keywords (`namespace=`, `targets=`/`defs=`, `flat=`, `preprocs=`) ride along exactly as on the call form:

```cmk {.snippet .language-cmk}
module Kit[|
  ping:; cmk.log(pong)
|]

Kit.import(namespace=Copied)     # == cmk.import.module(def=Kit namespace=Copied)
```

Only the `module`-keyword form is an instance; a bare `NAME[| .. |]` is a plain define, imported with the static `import.module(def=NAME)` above.

### Directives {% raw %}{#open-and-import-directives}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

In CMK-Lang the load is usually a **directive** rather than a raw `import.module` call, split by intent: `import` to *use* a module's members unqualified, `import ... as` to mount them under a prefix, and `open` to *contribute* members from the current file.  A bare macro imports the same way, then calls bare.  A soft compile-time lint (`CMK_NS_LINT=0` to silence) flags a mismatched intent, like an `import` you then define into, or an `open` that contributes nothing.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/module-import-open.cmk', github=github) }}

### Namespacing
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

On import, every **module-level** assignment and target name is prefixed with the namespace (`var1` → `MyModule.var1`).  A recipe reads its own module identity at runtime via the injected `CMK_MODULE` variable:

{% set ns_mk %}
define Greeter
greet:; $(call log, hi from $(CMK_MODULE))
._impl:; @echo module-local helper
endef
$(call import.module, def=Greeter)
#  greet   ->  Greeter.greet   (public: namespaced)
#  ._impl  ->  ._impl          (private: kept verbatim)
{% endset %}
{% set ns_cmk %}
Greeter[|
  greet:; cmk.log(hi from ${CMK_MODULE})
  ._impl:; @echo module-local helper
|]
cmk.import.module(def=Greeter)
#  greet   ->  Greeter.greet   (public: namespaced)
#  ._impl  ->  ._impl          (private: kept verbatim)
{% endset %}
{{site.inline_mirror('mirror_namespacing', ns_mk, ns_cmk, default='cmk')}}

!!! warning "Two things stay un-namespaced"
    * **Private members:** any name beginning with `.` or `_` (a `._impl` target, `.PHONY`) stays verbatim and module-local.
    * **Nested `define … endef` bodies:** literal text, passed through untouched.

Only the declared names are rewritten; dependencies, values, and recipe bodies are not.

### Plain Makefiles {% raw %}{#embedding-cmk-in-plain-makefiles}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Because the compile step runs at import time on a copy, a **vanilla Makefile** (stock `make -f`, no CMK shebang) can embed CMK-Lang in a `define` block and have just that block compiled on import.  The same module authored both ways -- a plain Makefile that embeds it in a `define`, and the CMK-Lang file where the body is native:

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/inlined-cmk.mk', 'demos/cmk/inlined-cmk.cmk', github=github, default='mk')}}

Only the `Report` body is compiled; the surrounding Makefile is left to stock `make`.

### From a File {% raw %}{#importing-from-a-file}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Instead of an inline `define`, a module can be an existing file:

{% set fromfile_mk %}
$(call import.module, file=path/to/module.mk namespace=mymod)
{% endset %}
{% set fromfile_cmk %}
cmk.import.module(file=path/to/module.mk namespace=mymod)
{% endset %}
{{site.inline_mirror('mirror_import_from_file', fromfile_mk, fromfile_cmk, default='cmk')}}

`def=` and `file=` are mutually exclusive (exactly one is required).  With `file=` and no `namespace=`, the module is named after the file's basename.

### Partial Imports
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

By default the whole module is imported.  For only part of one, add a `targets=` or `defs=` selector (mutually exclusive); each is a name or a glob (`*`/`?`):

{% set partial_mk %}
# only the targets matching the glob (still namespaced + compiled)
$(call import.module, def=MyModule targets='build.*')

# only the named define blocks
$(call import.module, file=lib.mk defs='helper_*')
{% endset %}
{% set partial_cmk %}
# only the targets matching the glob (still namespaced + compiled)
cmk.import.module(def=MyModule targets='build.*')

# only the named define blocks
cmk.import.module(file=lib.mk defs='helper_*')

# the same selector rides along on the import directive, for a plugin or file
# source (an inline def= block stays call-form, above):
import mymod, targets='build.*'      # only build.* (namespaced mymod.*)
import ./lib.mk, defs='helper_*'     # only the named defines
{% endset %}
{{site.inline_mirror('mirror_partial_imports', partial_mk, partial_cmk, default='cmk')}}

The selector is the first pipeline stage (`select → namespace → header → compile`), so chosen members are namespaced and compiled exactly as in a full import.  Selection is **by name only**: a selected target whose prerequisite is an unselected sibling is the caller's responsibility.  The same selector rides on the [`import` directive](#open-and-import-directives) too, for a plugin or file source.

### Flat Imports & Plugins {% raw %}{#flat-root-imports-and-the-plugin-connection}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Each pipeline stage is independently optional, so `import.module` spans a full matrix.  `flat=1` omits the namespace and `CMK_MODULE`-header stages, so the body lands in the **global namespace** (un-prefixed):

{% set flat_mk %}
# compile an inline CMK-Lang block straight into root (no prefix)
$(call import.module, def=MyBlock flat=1)

# flat + verbatim FILE -- just a plain include
$(call import.module, file=lib.mk flat=1 preprocs=stream.echo)
{% endset %}
{% set flat_cmk %}
# compile an inline CMK-Lang block straight into root (no prefix)
cmk.import.module(def=MyBlock flat=1)

# flat + verbatim FILE -- just a plain include
cmk.import.module(file=lib.mk flat=1 preprocs=stream.echo)
{% endset %}
{{site.inline_mirror('mirror_flat_imports', flat_mk, flat_cmk, default='cmk')}}

That last case (flat, verbatim, a file) is the spine's **fast-path**: nothing to transform, so the file is bound by a direct, copy-free `include`.  It is exactly what the [`include.*`]({{_}}/macros) verbs resolve to.  In other words, **a plugin is just a module with an empty pipeline**, two ends of one mechanism:

|                | verbatim (`preprocs=stream.echo`) | compiled (`preprocs=mk.compile`) |
|----------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------|
| **flat** (`flat=1`)   | a plain include / plugin          | compile a block into root        |
| **namespaced** | a namespaced verbatim include     | a module (the default)           |

#### cmk-lang plugins
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

The plugin verbs sit on the **flat** row and pick the *column by file extension*.  A plain `.mk` plugin takes the verbatim fast-path (a copy-free `include`); a `.cmk` plugin is **JIT-compiled** (lowered via `mk.compile`) and included at root; so a plugin can be written in CMK-Lang and is lowered at include time, whether you arrived via `make -f` or `cmk run`.  The same `include.plugins` call, from a plain `Makefile` and from its cmk-lang mirror:

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/plugin-system.mk', 'demos/cmk/plugin-system.cmk', github=github, default='cmk')}}

`strict=0` makes a missing plugin a no-op for both extensions.

### Executable Modules {% raw %}{#executable-modules}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

A module or plugin can be **runnable and importable at once** (Pythonic, like `if __name__ == "__main__"`).  Give it a [`__main__:`]({{_}}/config#dunder-names-__name__) rule:

```cmk {.snippet .language-cmk}
#!/usr/bin/env -S ./compose.mk cmk run
'''tools.cmk: a runnable, importable module'''
__main__:; cmk.log(tools ${sep} try build or test)
build:; @echo building
test:;  @echo testing
```

* **Run it directly** (`./tools.cmk`, via the `cmk run` shebang) and `__main__` is the default goal.
* **Import it** (`cmk.include.plugins(tools.cmk)`) and that `__main__` is **demoted** to a plain `<name>:` target (here, `tools`) so it never collides with the importer's own `__main__`; running the `tools` target then prints its overview.

The demotion happens at import time, with nothing extra to declare.  The stdlib's `json.cmk` is written exactly this way; it and the rest of the shipped modules are catalogued in the [modules index]({{_}}/plugins/overview/#index-modules).

### Declaration Family {% raw %}{#the-declaration-family}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

`import.module` is one member of a broader **declaration** namespace: parse-time verbs that bring definitions into scope before any recipe runs.  Its siblings bring other kinds of things into scope by the same convention: single defines, targets, plugins, compose files, and polyglots.  The `declare.*` namespace collects the parse-time registrars created by constructors.

{{site.block_docs('DeclarationIndex', mkdocs)}}

#### Reading a signature {% raw %}{#reading-a-signature}{% endraw %}
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

The **brings-into-scope** column above is a compact arglist/optlist synopsis.
Each token has a fixed meaning:

{{ site.signature_legend() }}

> **Marquee signatures.**  The same styling, applied to a handful of real declaration members:
> {: .cmk-card }
>
> <dl class="sig-list">
>   <dt>import.module</dt>
>   <dd>{{ site.signature('def=<name>|file=<path> [namespace=] [targets=/defs= for partial]') }}</dd>
>   <dt>include.plugin</dt>
>   <dd>{{ site.signature('<file..> from CMK_PLUGINS_DIR [strict=0 -> log+continue]') }}</dd>
>   <dt>import.def</dt>
>   <dd>{{ site.signature('file=<path> def=<name> [as=] [namespace=] (one define)') }}</dd>
>   <dt>import.target</dt>
>   <dd>{{ site.signature('file=<path> target=<name> [as=] [namespace=]') }}</dd>
>   <dt>vm.reflect</dt>
>   <dd>{{ site.signature('prefix=<p> | exclude=<prefixes>') }}</dd>
> </dl>

A **required key** (`def=`) must be supplied; a **placeholder** (`<name>`, or
`<file..>` for a repeatable one) is where your value goes; anything in
**`[ brackets ]`** is optional; `|` marks alternative forms; `->` reads as
*"then does"*.

